    "basis": "reconstruction",
    "fidelity": "not-applicable",
    "note": "The finished design was read from Brian Wichmann’s C129A drawing, its corners snapped to exact values. We devised the construction: one line from K1 through E fixes P, and every line is drawn through P, S, T, U, W or Q. The drawing gives no steps.",
    "changes": "The compass steps, their order and the named points are ours. The comparison with the drawing checks the finished lines, not how the design was first laid out. Castéra’s picture is uncoloured; the colours are ours. No photo of the madrasa was checked.",
    "reviewed": "2026-10-26"
  },
  "credits": [
    {
      "source": "Tiling Search, data152/C129A: Merinid Madrasah, Sale",
      "creator": "Brian Wichmann",
      "url": "https://tilingsearch.mit.edu/HTML/data152/C129A.html",
      "kind": "drawing",
      "note": "The design was read from his drawing. He takes it from Jean-Marc Castéra, Arabesques: Decorative Art in Morocco (ACR Édition, Paris, 1999), page 129, top (Merinid madrasa, Salé, uncoloured in the book). The construction is ours."
    }
